.TH LANDER 6 2026-10-29 "Lunar Lander" "Games Manual"
.SH NAME
lander \- land Eagle the fat cat on the Moon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lander
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lander
is a classic arcade lunar lander with vector graphics and synthesized
sound.
The pilot is Eagle, a very round cat with excellent whiskers and a
rocket strapped to his backside.
Steer him down through gravity, wind and rough terrain onto a landing
pad, softly and upright, without running out of fuel.
.PP
The game opens in a 960\(mu640 window.
It takes no command-line options.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP 14
.BR Left / Right ", " A / D
Rotate Eagle anticlockwise / clockwise.
.TP
.BR Up ", " W
Fire the rocket along Eagle's body axis.
.TP
.B Space
Start a normal run (title screen).
.TP
.B D
Start today's daily run (title screen); see
.BR "DAILY MOON" .
.TP
.B P
Pause or resume.
.TP
.B R
After a landing, fly the next level.
After a crash, retry the same moon if you have lives left; otherwise
end the run.
.TP
.B Esc
End the current run.
On the title screen, quit.
.TP
.BR "A\(enZ" ", " "0\(en9" ", " Backspace ", " Return
Type your name for the high-score table.
.SH LANDING
A touchdown counts as a landing only if
.IP \(bu 3
both feet are on the same green pad,
.IP \(bu
descent speed is under 28 and sideways drift under 18,
.IP \(bu
Eagle is within about 11\(de of upright.
.PP
Anything else is a crash, and so is any part of Eagle other than his
feet touching rock.
The world wraps horizontally: fly off one edge and you come back on the
other.
.SH SCORING
A landing scores 50 \(mu the pad's multiplier, plus the fuel left in the
tank.
Narrower pads carry higher multipliers:
.BR 2X " (90 px wide) through " 5X " (45 px)."
From level 4 the 2X pad is replaced by a 5X.
.PP
The eight best runs are kept on the high-score table.
.SH FUEL AND LIVES
Fuel carries over from level to level.
Each landing refuels 20 + 5 \(mu the pad multiplier, up to a full tank.
Eagle gets lighter as he burns fuel, so the rocket kicks harder on a
near-empty tank.
.PP
A run starts with three lives and can hold up to nine.
A crash costs one, and you retry the same moon with the fuel you started
it with.
A landing on a 4X pad or better earns a life back.
The run ends when the last life is gone, or when you press
.BR Esc .
.SH DIFFICULTY
Each level brings rougher terrain, stronger gravity and narrower pads.
.TP 14
.B Level 3+
Wind: a steady breeze from one side with gusts on top.
The arrow at the top of the screen shows its direction and strength.
.TP
.B Level 5+
A floating
.B 6X
pad swings from side to side.
To land on it, match its speed as you touch down; Eagle then rides
along with it.
Flying into its underside is a crash.
.SH HEADS-UP DISPLAY
.TP 14
.B F
Fuel, with a bar gauge.
.TP
.B VH
Horizontal speed (positive is to the right).
.TP
.B VV
Vertical speed (positive is downwards).
.TP
.B ALT
Height of Eagle's feet above whatever is directly below him.
.TP
Tilt gauge
The needle shows Eagle's axis and the ticks mark the legal landing
tilt.
.PP
A reading turns red when it is outside landing limits.
On the right are the score, the level and one cat head per life.
.PP
Below about 90 px of altitude the camera zooms in 2\(mu, and it zooms
back out above about 130 px.
.SH DAILY MOON
Pressing
.B D
on the title screen starts a run on the daily moon: the run seed is
today's date, so everyone playing that day flies the same sequence of
moons.
.PP
Your best landing on each daily level is saved, and the next time you fly
that level it replays as a translucent blue
.I ghost
Eagle alongside you.
The physics is deterministic, so the ghost retraces that flight
exactly.
Ghosts are kept for the current day only.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/lander/hiscores.txt
The high-score table: one
.RI \(dq name\~score \(dq
pair per line.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/lander/ghost.txt
Today's daily ghosts, stored as run-length-encoded keyboard input.
.PP
If
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset or not an absolute path,
.I ~/.local/share
is used.
The directory is created on first use.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base directory for the save files; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.BR SDL_VIDEODRIVER ", " SDL_AUDIODRIVER
Passed through to SDL, for example
.B SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy
to play silently.
Without a usable audio device the game runs silent on its own.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a normal quit; 1 if SDL cannot open a window or renderer.
.SH NOTES
The same game builds to WebAssembly and runs in a browser, where scores
and ghosts are kept in the browser's IndexedDB storage instead of files.
.PP
Everything is drawn with lines, and every sound is synthesized live;
the game itself needs no image or sound files.
